    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's a summary of the change I implemented.\n\n## What the spec required\n\n`POST /api/owners` must record, and `GET /api/owners/{id}` must return, `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners (before this create) whose `firstName` and `lastName` match the new owner's, compared case-insensitively. Since the count is read back later via GET and is defined \"before this create\", it must be computed at creation time and persisted on the owner.\n\n## Changes\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field (`namesake_count` column) with getter/setter.\n\n2. **DB schemas** \u2014 added `namesake_count INTEGER` to the `owners` table in all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests, h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n3. **OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a `readOnly` `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it automatically.\n\n4. **New OfficeFloor function** `AssignNamesakeCount` \u2014 counts existing owners sharing the same first+last name (case-insensitive, null-safe) via `OwnerRepository.findAll()` and sets the count on the entity. It runs *before* save, mirroring the existing `AssignCustomerCode` step so the new owner isn't counted.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted `assignNamesakeCount` (under `transaction` governance) between `assignCustomerCode` and `save`.\n\nThe `coreCountsNamesakes` acceptance test \u2014 creating two owners with the same name but different postcodes, then asserting the second owner's `namesakeCount == 1` \u2014 passes, as does the full suite.",
